Damien Neil wrote on Thu, 16 Dec 2004 16:09:14 -0800:
> In article <MPG.1c2ac69495930b5098a2c0@216.168.3.44>, Rast
> <rast2@hotmail.com> wrote:

> > The purpose of keeping score in a single player game is to give players 
> > a means to compare their performances.  I think people have lost sight 
> > of that.

> I don't think conducts should be used in calculating the score, for a
> couple reasons.  First off, I like them as purely optional tasks--if
> you *want* to play a vegan atheist, you can, but you should never feel
> compelled to do so just to push your rank up higher on the high score
> table. 

If a challenging conduct doesn't push your score upwards, why should 
anything else?  Score is supposed to measure how well the player did; 
otherwise it is meaningless.

Take Pacman as an example.  You get points for eating dots, points for 
eating ghosts, and points for eating the occasional fruit.  The game ends 
when you run out of lives.  The scoring system is well balanced; if two 
people each play one game, the one who scored higher played better, and 
even the loser will acknowledge that (or complain about the joystick).

A proper scoring system will give a higher score to the player who has a 
better game.  If you aren't going to attempt that, why tinker with the 
score at all?  We already have a scoring system that doesn't reward 
skill; we don't need a more different scoring system that still doesn't 
reward skill.

> Secondly, it's going to be virtually impossible to properly
> balance the difficulty of the different conducts

Difficult, but not even close to impossible.  Actually ascending a seven-
conduct monstrosity -- now *that* is virtually impossible.

> Turns used is a good one--a fast ascention is certainly more impressive
> than a slow one..  (It was, in fact, one of the criteria I proposed, so
> there's at least one of them that you don't disagree with.)  I don't
> think that it works as the sole factor in scoring, however, unless you
> want to reduce the score to (base score - turns used), which doesn't
> sound very entertaining.  Score, to me, is more interesting when you
> need to balance multiple factors against each other: "Should I try to
> kill off a few more demon lords, or am I better off sprinting for the
> exit?"

The answer is going to be one or the other.  If it is more rewarding to 
kill monsters, the player is going to do that -- repeatedly.  If it is 
more rewarding to sprint for the exit, why give any points at all for 
monster kills?


Let's look at just one area of the game for an example: the zoo in 
Rodney's tower.  You almost always have to go through it to ascend[1]. 
There are many ways to beat the zoo:

- Stealth-ily carve a path through the sleeping monsters with your 
weapon/spell/rubber chicken of choice
- blow up the room with expert-level fireballs
- ring of Conflict, and walk right through 
- lock your pet Archon in and let him do it
- kill the zoo with many scrolls of genocide

Who do you think should earn the most points in the zoo?  My answer: the 
player who goes from the entrance to the exit in the fewest turns, 
whomever that is[2].  Rewarding monster kills makes solving the zoo 
*less* skill-intensive. 


> Finally, I don't think that progress towards ascention should be the
> sole component of the score.  As I see it, most games (not just
> NetHack) have several stages of play:
>  - Can I win?
>  - Can I win consistently?
>  - How high can I score while winning?
>  - Can I win with one hand tied behind my back?
> 
> At the first two stages, when a player cannot reliably ascend, score is
> mostly irrelevent.  The player is focused on winning the game, not
> optimizing the score of a losing game.

But that simply isn't true.  It is the new player who is most obsessed 
with score.  That is why when one dies he mentions that it was his best 
score yet, and why when he ascends for the first time he brings along a 
bagful of gems and gold.


